task: cbor-decode-threshold-trampoline
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/cbor-decode-threshold-trampoline
worktree: ../okay-wt-cbor-trampoline
spec: specs/iterative-recursive-decode.md (root 1 of 2: Cbor.get)

why: the operator asked to start the implementation the spec describes.
  Scope for THIS lane: `Cbor.get` only — one of the two recursive
  roots the spec names. `Json.decode` and `JsonStrict.Reader.get` are
  separate follow-up lanes (BACKLOG stays open for them), kept apart
  because Json.decode has no existing depth counter to reuse (its
  depth is bounded upstream by Json.isCut, differently) and mixing
  three decoders' worth of changes into one lane is exactly how
  TestCompat's Wire removal drifted out of sync before.

what: `Cbor.get` gains a native/trampoline split at `in.depth` (the
  EXISTING enter/leave counter, read-only accessor added, no second
  counter): below a small threshold, today's code unchanged
  (renamed `getNative`); at or past it, a newly-written `getC`
  produces a `Cont`-based computation via `Cont.defer` at the ONE
  point each case recurses into a nested schema, `reset` at the
  boundary. `Codecs.maxDepth`'s refusal (`enter`/`tooDeep`) is
  preserved identically in both paths — the threshold changes HOW
  depth is paid for, not what depth is allowed.

predict: shallow (below-threshold) documents show no measurable
  regression (a JMH-quality benchmark gates this, not a hand-timed
  loop — the spec's own Behavior checklist item); deep/pathological
  documents decode instead of overflowing, up to Codecs.maxDepth
  raised back toward its pre-lower-maxdepth-real-margin range as a
  FOLLOW-UP decision once this lane's numbers are in, not assumed here.
